---
import CasePage from '../../components/case/CasePage.astro';
import Section from '../../components/case/Section.astro';
import Plate from '../../components/case/Plate.astro';
import Limits from '../../components/case/Limits.astro';
import VerdictTrace from '../../components/case/figures/VerdictTrace.astro';
---

<CasePage
  num="01"
  domain="Legal retrieval"
  title="VERDICT"
  subtitle="A legal RAG system that refuses to invent a case."
  abstract="Indian Supreme Court judgments are long, cross-referenced, and unforgiving of a wrong citation. VERDICT retrieves at the paragraph level, generates from the document level, and declines to answer when the evidence is not there."
  description="VERDICT: parent–child legal retrieval over 1,000+ Supreme Court judgments, with fuzzy case routing and a semantic refusal threshold."
  year="2026"
  status="Repository · Retrieval system"
  stack="Python · ChromaDB · Streamlit"
  spec={[
    { label: 'Problem', value: 'An answer that cites a case must cite it correctly.' },
    { label: 'Constraint', value: 'Judgments run to hundreds of pages; embedding windows cut the reasoning away from the holding.' },
    { label: 'Approach', value: 'Parent–child chunking, fuzzy case routing, a semantic refusal threshold, and two generation modes.' },
    { label: 'Result', value: 'A working Streamlit system over 1,000+ judgments, with the ingestion and retrieval pipeline in the repository.' },
  ]}
  links={[
    { label: 'Code', href: 'https://github.com/AmarthyaT/Verdict-Legal_Judgment_RAG' },
    { label: 'Summarizer', href: 'https://huggingface.co/Amarthya11/Llama3.1-finetuned-GGUF' },
  ]}
  contents={[
    { id: 'question', label: 'The question' },
    { id: 'method', label: 'The method' },
    { id: 'refusal', label: 'What it refuses' },
    { id: 'result', label: 'The result' },
    { id: 'limits', label: 'Known limits' },
  ]}
  sources={[
    { label: 'Repository', note: 'Pipeline, evaluation suite, commit history.' },
    { label: 'Guardrail', note: '0.75 distance threshold, surfaced in the UI.' },
    { label: 'Llama 3.1 GGUF', note: 'QLoRA summarizer, Q4_K_M and Q8_0.' },
  ]}
  next={{ num: '02', title: 'TB-AMR', href: '/work/tb-amr' }}
>
  <Section id="question" num="01" label="The question" title="A wrong citation is worse than no answer.">
    <p>
      Ask a general model for a Supreme Court precedent and it will often give you one. The name will
      sound plausible. The citation will be wrong. The failure is not that the model is ignorant of
      law; it is that legal writing is long, cross-referential and structured, and a short embedding
      window cuts the reasoning away from the holding.
    </p>
    <p>
      VERDICT was built around a narrower question: can retrieval be made to carry the burden of
      proof? If the system cannot find a passage it can point to, it should not answer.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="method" num="02" label="The method" title="Retrieve small. Generate from the whole.">
    <p>
      Judgments are chunked twice. Child chunks — paragraph-level — are embedded and searched; the
      parent document is what the generator reads. Retrieval precision comes from the child,
      narrative coherence from the parent.
    </p>
    <p>
      Queries take two routes. If the question names a case, an LLM extracts the name and a Token Set
      Ratio match against a catalog of more than 1,000 Supreme Court entries routes straight to the
      whole judgment. If it does not, semantic search returns the nearest child chunks.
    </p>
    <p>
      The guardrail is a distance. At a threshold of 0.75 the system either answers from retrieved
      text or declines and produces a doctrinal overview instead. A separate path catches fictional
      cases and grounds the analysis in Article 14, natural justice and proportionality without
      pretending the case exists.
    </p>
    <p>
      Generation runs in two modes: Ollama with Llama 3 8B and <span class="mono-note">bge-base-en-v1.5</span> entirely
      local, or Gemini 2.5 Flash for synthesis with local embeddings. The digest follows an
      eleven-section template.
    </p>

    <Fragment slot="figure">
      <Plate
        num="01"
        title="Routing and refusal"
        caption="One query, three exits. The system only synthesises when a retrieved passage can support the sentence; otherwise it says so and changes register."
      >
        <VerdictTrace />
      </Plate>
    </Fragment>
  </Section>

  <Section id="refusal" num="03" label="What it refuses" title="A refusal is only useful if it is legible.">
    <p>
      The dashboard shows the ranked chunk distances, the route the query took, and the exact context
      passed to the model. A wrong answer can therefore be traced to a retrieval step instead of
      argued about at the output.
    </p>
    <p>
      The fictional-case path is deliberately not a refusal. A hypothetical bench test is a real way
      to probe doctrine; the system answers it as doctrine and labels it hypothetical. That
      distinction — unknown case versus invented case — is the whole design in one decision.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="result" num="04" label="The result" title="What actually runs.">
    <p>
      VERDICT is a Streamlit application over a Chroma store of more than 1,000 Supreme Court
      judgments, with an instruction-tuned Llama 3.1 8B — QLoRA, two GGUF quantisations — as the
      local summariser.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Local mode: Ollama, Llama 3 8B, BGE embeddings, no network call.</li>
      <li>Cloud mode: Gemini 2.5 Flash for synthesis, local retrieval unchanged.</li>
      <li>Routing: fuzzy case match at Token Set Ratio ≥ 75, then semantic search.</li>
      <li>Guardrail: a 0.75 distance threshold, surfaced in the interface rather than hidden.</li>
    </ul>
  </Section>

  <Section id="limits" num="05" label="Known limits">
    <Limits>
      <p>
        Citation accuracy is not published here. The evaluation harness generates synthetic RAGAS
        pairs, and a synthetic set is not a lawyer's judgment of a correct citation. Until a blind
        review exists, what is demonstrated is the pipeline's behaviour — routing, refusal,
        provenance — not its legal correctness.
      </p>
      <p>
        The corpus is Supreme Court only. High Court reasoning, repeals and amendments sit outside
        it, and a confident answer about a repealed provision is exactly the failure the distance
        guardrail cannot catch.
      </p>
      <p>
        The repository ships the ingestion and retrieval code, not a prebuilt index. Reproducing the
        corpus means running ingestion against your own judgment PDFs, which is hours of work and
        an embedding bill, not a download.
      </p>
      <p>
        Case-name extraction is itself an LLM call. A misread name falls through to semantic search,
        which is survivable, but it means two probabilistic steps stand in front of the evidence.
      </p>
    </Limits>
  </Section>
</CasePage>
